aving six vertices, ten edges, and five triangles.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="IJTA2008-210471.005"/></fig><fig id="fig6" position="float"><label>Figure 6</label><caption><p>(a) A Voronoi celland (b) the Voronoi diagram of a number of sites.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="IJTA2008-210471.006"/></fig><fig id="fig7" position="float"><label>Figure 7</label><caption><p>A Voronoi diagram (dashed) and its straight line dual,the Delaunay triangulation.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="IJTA2008-210471.007"/></fig><fig id="fig8" position="float"><label>Figure 8</label><caption><p>(a)  A contour. Itis evident that triangle <italic>e</italic>, <italic>f</italic>, <italic>g</italic> cannot be apart of a Delaunay triangulation, since its circumcircle would contain anothersite. (b) shows theDelaunay triangulation of the contour points. (c)  Contour edge <italic>e</italic>, <italic>f</italic> is containedafter addition of a vertex to. (d) Elimination ofexterior edges, added vertex, and the edges related to this vertex.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="IJTA2008-210471.008"/></fig><fig id="fig9" position="float"><label>Figure 9</label><caption><p>Two parallel contours and 50 pointson each one.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="IJTA2008-210471.009"/></fig><fig id="fig10" position="float"><label>Figure 10</label><caption><p>Starting point <italic>A</italic><sub>0</sub>, the nearest point in the adjacent contour <italic>B</italic><sub>50</sub>, the nearest neighborhood of <italic>A</italic><sub>0</sub> in theclockwise direction <italic>A</italic><sub>1</sub>, and the triangle <italic>A</italic><sub>0</sub><italic>A</italic><sub>1</sub><italic>B</italic><sub>50</sub>.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="IJTA2008-210471.010"/></fig><fig id="fig11" position="float"><label>Figure 11</label><caption><p>Triangle <italic>A</italic><sub>0</sub><italic>A</italic><sub>1</sub><italic>B</italic><sub>50</sub>,  triangle <italic>A</italic><sub>1</sub><italic>A</italic><sub>2</sub><italic>B</italic><sub>51</sub>, and the triangle <italic>B</italic><sub>50</sub><italic>B</italic><sub>51</sub><italic>A</italic><sub>1</sub> that formsbetween them.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="IJTA2008-210471.011"/></fig><fig id="fig12" position="float"><label>Figure 12</label><caption><p>Triangulation <italic>T</italic><sub>0</sub> between twocontours (sections).</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="IJTA2008-210471.012"/></fig><fig id="fig13" position="float"><label>Figure 13</label><caption><p>					<italic>A</italic><sub>0</sub>: starting point.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="IJTA2008-210471.013"/></fig><fig id="fig14" position="float"><label>Figure 14</label><caption><p>					<italic>A</italic>					<sub>24</sub>: starting point&#x02014;selection of the besttriangulation. </p></caption><graphic xlink:href="IJTA2008-210471.014"/></fig><fig id="fig15" position="float"><label>Figure 15</label><caption><p>Reconstruction of braintumor: 50 points, 100 points, and 200 points on the contours.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="IJTA2008-210471.015"/></fig><fig id="fig16" position="float"><label>Figure 16</label><caption><p>The area of <italic>T</italic> is the sum ofthe areas <italic>t</italic><sub>1</sub>, <italic>t</italic><sub>2</sub>, and <italic>t</italic><sub>3</sub>. </p></caption><graphic xlink:href="IJTA2008-210471.016"/></fig><fig id="fig17" position="float"><label>Figure 17</label><caption><p>The angle at <italic>p</italic>.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="IJTA2008-210471.017"/></fig><fig id="fig18" position="float"><label>Figure 18</label><caption><p>Dihedral angle of the edge.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="IJTA2008-210471.018"/></fig><fig id="fig19" position="float"><label>Figure 19</label><caption><p>Patient 1. (a) 50 points on thecontours, (b) 100 points on thecontours, (c) 200 points on thecontours, and (d) 400 points on thecontours.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="IJTA2008-210471.019"/></fig><fig id="fig20" position="float"><label>Figure 20</label><caption><p>Patient 1. (a) 50 points on thecontours, (b) 100 points on thecontours, and (c) 200 points on thecontours.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="IJTA2008-210471.020"/></fig><fig id="fig21" position="float"><label>Figure 21</label><caption><p>Patient 1. (a) 50 points on thecontours, (b) 100 points on thecontours, (c) 200 points on thecontours, and (d) 400 points on thecontours.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="IJTA2008-210471.021"/></fig><fig id="fig22" position="float"><label>Figure 22</label><caption><p>Patient 2. (a) 50 points on thecontours, (b) 100 points on thecontours, (c) 200 points on thecontours, and (d) 400 points on thecontours.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="IJTA2008-210471.022"/></fig><fig id="fig23" position="float"><label>Figure 23</label><caption><p>Patient 2. (a) 50 points on thecontours, (b) 100 points on thecontours, and (c) 200 points on thecontours.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="IJTA2008-210471.023"/></fig><fig id="fig24" position="float"><label>Figure 24</label><caption><p>Patient 2. (a) 50 points on thecontours, (b) 100 points on thecontours, and (c) 200 points on thecontours.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="IJTA2008-210471.024"/></fig><fig id="fig25" position="float"><label>Figure 25</label><caption><p>Patient 2. (a) 50 points on thecontours, (b) 100 points on thecontours, and (c) 200 points on thecontours.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="IJTA2008-210471.025"/></fig><fig id="fig26" position="float"><label>Figure 26</label><caption><p>Patient 2. (a) 50 points on thecontours, (b) 100 points on thecontours, and (c) 200 points on thecontours.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="IJTA2008-210471.026"/></fig><fig id="fig27" position="float"><label>Figure 27</label><caption><p>Patient 2. (a) 50 points on thecontours, (b) 100 points on thecontours, and (c) 200 points on thecontours.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="IJTA2008-210471.027"/></fig><fig id="fig28" position="float"><label>Figure 28</label><caption><p>Patient 3. (a) 50 points o